Q: How do I change font of comments in visual studio code? A: <p>This is now possible with unmodified VS Code, since <a href="https://github.com/Microsoft/vscode/issues/36512" rel="nofollow noreferrer">1.126.0</a>. For example, to use the Monaspace Argon and Xenon fonts, you can add the following to your JSON settings:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
    &quot;editor.fontFamily&quot;: &quot;'Monaspace Argon Var'&quot;,
    &quot;editor.tokenColorCustomizations&quot;: {
        &quot;textMateRules&quot;: [
            {
                &quot;scope&quot;: &quot;comment.line.documentation&quot;,
                &quot;settings&quot;: {
                    &quot;fontFamily&quot;: &quot;'Monaspace Xenon Var'&quot;
                }
            }
        ]
    },
}
</code></pre>
<p>To figure out what value of <code>&quot;scope&quot;</code> to apply your settings to, use the <code>Developer: Inspect Editor Tokens and Scopes</code> command, move your cursor to the text that you want to style, and look at the <code>textmate scopes</code>. These can be shortened to apply more generally - for example a Rust doc comment is <code>comment.line.documentation.rust</code>, but is matched by the <code>comment.line.documentation</code> scope above.</p>

Q: Can you search a HTMLElement for a particular class? A: <p><code>getElementsByClassName()</code> can be called on <strong>any element</strong>, not just <code>document</code>.</p>
<pre><code>const parent = document.getElementsByClassName(&quot;class_1&quot;)[0];
const children = parent.getElementsByClassName(&quot;class_2&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Or, using <code>querySelector</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const parent = document.querySelector(&quot;.class_1&quot;);
const children = parent.querySelectorAll(&quot;.class_2&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Both examples return only the <code>.class_2</code> elements that are inside the first <code>.class_1</code> element.</p>
